# Voxel Flyover: a corgi on a jet surfboard, a pelican on a bicycle pedalling under it for the
# jets, flies over a planet that can be left, a sphere of 600,000 units
# drawn by Godot's Forward+ renderer on gpu@0. Its world is a function its shaders compute at
# the points of the sphere: a pass draws the ground into textures as the flight goes, and the
# vertex stages raise grids of land to it, out to the horizon and round the planet. Its project
# (demos/godot/voxel-flyover) is imported and exported here by Godot's editor, and voxel-flyover
# plays the pack with godot, which installs with it. The project installs too.
FROM /v0.1.6/Dollyfile-system 33e5bcf65ef4a634da5dbf8e291a379148e7971aa0411fe1bdaa4e0332f24b94
INSTALL /v0.1.6/demos/godot/Dollyfile-godot d664727847aab2605329b43c8b088d8f933d61ec3717f24554c0c9ce703c053c
USE /v0.1.6/demos/godot/Dollyfile-godot-editor 6dd0e5b78f2e4bd5b03d170727bc0a77aa734c49af7bad280263005696a0a52a

REQUIRES TOOL cp
REQUIRES TOOL godot-editor
REQUIRES TOOL mkdir
REQUIRES TOOL rm
REQUIRES TOOL tar

SOURCE /v0.1.6/dist/static/godot/voxel-flyover.tar 5a55cf42a36415ab202f0139e69928892ffbe6728f236f2bc6f68d109e29152d /tmp/voxel-flyover.tar
SLOP CWD / tar -xf /tmp/voxel-flyover.tar
SLOP mkdir -p /usr/share/voxel-flyover
SLOP cp -r /usr/src/voxel-flyover /usr/share/voxel-flyover/project
SLOP CWD /usr/src/voxel-flyover godot-editor --headless --import
SLOP CWD /usr/src/voxel-flyover godot-editor --headless --export-pack Dolly /usr/share/voxel-flyover/voxel-flyover.pck
SLOP rm -rf /usr/src/voxel-flyover /tmp/voxel-flyover.tar

FILE /usr/bin/voxel-flyover
    #!/bin/slop
    godot --main-pack /usr/share/voxel-flyover/voxel-flyover.pck "$@"
EXPORTS TOOL voxel-flyover
FILE /usr/share/man/cat1/voxel-flyover.1
    voxel-flyover [GODOT-OPTION...] [-- --autopilot SECONDS] [-- --day HOUR] [-- --tour SECOND]
    Plays Voxel Flyover on the page: a corgi in a cape and sunglasses on a
    jet surfboard, a pelican on a bicycle pedalling under it for the jets,
    over a planet 600,000 units in radius, through its days and nights. The land runs on to a curved horizon, and far ranges rise
    over it as the board nears them. It flies itself until Enter or a click
    takes the board: arrows or WASD steer, climb and dive, T lets the hours
    run, Escape lets go of the board, Q quits. A click also gives the game
    the pointer, and the mouse then turns the view; the wheel, or Z and X,
    zoom. A board that keeps climbing is faster the higher it is: it leaves
    the clouds under it, the sky goes dark and the stars come out, and from
    some hundred thousand units up the planet is seen whole, with its day
    and night sides, its clouds, its seas' glint, its towns' lights and the
    rim of its air. Diving brings it back down, anywhere on the planet. A
    lap along the ground would take 35 hours. On a weak GPU it draws less,
    at an even rate. The number on the screen counts the beacons lit. With
    --autopilot it flies by itself for that long and prints its frame rate,
    how even its frames were, the milliseconds a frame took the GPU and the
    renderer, the beacons it lit, how high it went and what its frame
    shows; --day starts at that hour; --tour leaves the planet at that
    second, to the height --top gives (900000), and comes back. It draws
    through WebGPU (gpu@0). Its project is /usr/share/voxel-flyover/project:
    copy it to edit it with godot-editor.
FILE /usr/share/voxel-flyover/voxel-flyover.pck
FOLDER /usr/share/voxel-flyover/project
